# Codex permission rules. Tracked deliberately: an untracked allow-list is per-machine, so
# two operators get different agent behaviour from the same task.
# Mirrors TIER 1 of the STANDING AUTHORIZATION block on every chart-backlog bead. Tiers 2 and 3
# are per-bead grants and human gates, deliberately NOT here; baking them into a machine-wide
# prefix rule erases the distinction.
#
# THE BAR IS "BOUNDED AND RECOVERABLE", NOT "READ-ONLY AND HERMETIC". Every header here asserted
# the latter until 2026-08-31 and it was false of nearly every file: most of these rules reach
# the network, and several WRITE. Each file states its own writes below. Nothing here pushes a
# git ref, mutates a cloud provider or a cluster, or merges a PR -- but bd DOES write shared
# state, so read bd.rules before assuming a grant is local.
#
# A rule constrains a command's PREFIX and nothing else -- never a flag, path or refspec that
# appears later in argv. If you cannot express the safe subset as a prefix, do not write the
# allow. Every defect found in review was a comment asserting an invariant prefix matching
# cannot enforce.
# Added 2026-08-31.
# Network: every rule here talks to the cluster API. None of them mutates it.
#
# REMOVED: 'view' from the config alternation. kubectl config view --raw matches the prefix and
# DUMPS CLIENT CERTIFICATES AND TOKENS from the kubeconfig. Forbidding --raw is bypassable
# (kubectl config view --minify --raw).
#
# RESIDUAL, stated rather than hidden: kubectl get also matches kubectl get secrets -o yaml.
# The resource name sits later in argv, so no prefix rule can exclude it -- the same
# argv-in-the-middle problem as bd update <id> --notes. If sessions here hold cluster
# credentials, guard it in a hook; a rule cannot.
#
# REMOVED 2026-09-01: "cluster-info" from the union. `kubectl cluster-info dump` matches the
# prefix and emits full cluster state plus pod logs, which routinely carry credentials -- the
# same credential-exposure class as `config view` above. The `dump` sits later in argv.
prefix_rule(pattern=["kubectl", ["get", "describe", "logs", "explain", "api-resources", "api-versions", "version", "top"]], decision="allow")
prefix_rule(pattern=["kubectl", "auth", "can-i"], decision="allow")
prefix_rule(pattern=["kubectl", "config", ["current-context", "get-contexts"]], decision="allow")
